  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{25y}{4}=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\frac{5}{2}$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha {e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha {e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{\u2032},$ and ${y}_{p}^{\u2033}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha {e}^{-x}-5(-\\alpha {e}^{-x})+\\frac{25}{4}\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ (1+5+\\frac{25}{4})\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{49}{4}\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{49}{4}\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{16}{49}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}.\n$$",
